Draw the **Lattice Assembler**, an 8-frame **sprite sheet** of a chunky,
pseudo-3D industrial crafting machine, using only the drawing tool.

The Lattice Assembler is the **3×3 crafting machine** in Lattice, a deterministic
factory simulation rendered in **Factorio's high-angle, pseudo-3D style**: it
occupies a 3×3 block of tiles, consumes inputs, and counts up crafting ticks to
produce an output. You are drawing the *machine* and its working/craft animation —
not the items it crafts and not the belts around it. It must read as a **raised
block with real height** (a lit top face, beveled sides, a grounding contact
shadow), not a flat top-down plate — the brief's **The look** section is the thing
to get right.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. It
is the sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue — recorded to a per-frame action log — are the **authoritative output that
is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced any other way (writing a
PNG directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the
recorded operations count.

Each frame is its **own separate 96×96 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 4 --y 4 --width 88 --height 88 --color "#4d5a64"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

